---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatColor from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatColor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hex-to-cmyk">
  <DevFormatColor client:load from="hex" to="cmyk" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The HEX to CMYK formula and its limits</h2>
    <p>
      This is the naive, device-independent HEX to CMYK conversion. Design software uses the same
      arithmetic when colour management is off.
    </p>
    <pre><code>#2563eb → r=0.145 g=0.388 b=0.922 (normalised)

K = 1 - max(r, g, b)        = 1 - 0.922 = 0.078 → 7.8%
C = (1 - r - K) / (1 - K)   = 0.844 → 84.4%
M = (1 - g - K) / (1 - K)   = 0.577 → 57.7%
Y = (1 - b - K) / (1 - K)   = 0.000 → 0%

cmyk(84.4%, 57.7%, 0%, 7.8%)</code></pre>
    <p>
      The formula does not know the paper, the ink set, the press, the dot gain or the colour profile. A
      real conversion is a lookup through an ICC profile that encodes all of those. Use these numbers as
      a start point and an approximation, not as artwork values.
    </p>

    <h2>Why K exists</h2>
    <p>
      In theory, 100% cyan plus 100% magenta plus 100% yellow absorbs all light and gives black. In
      practice it gives a muddy dark brown, uses three times the ink, soaks the paper until it cockles,
      and takes much longer to dry. So black is printed with its own plate (the K in CMYK, for
      &ldquo;key&rdquo;) and subtracted from the other three. That is why the formula computes K first.
    </p>
    <p>
      For body text, use 100% K only. Four-colour black on small text shows registration fringes if the
      plates are a fraction of a millimetre out.
    </p>

    <h2>Out-of-gamut colours</h2>
    <p>
      sRGB and CMYK cover different regions of visible colour. sRGB includes bright saturated cyans,
      greens, oranges and pure blues that no ink combination can print. Those colours are &ldquo;out of
      gamut&rdquo;: the conversion returns the closest printable colour, which is duller and often has a
      different hue.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Screen colour</th><th>What happens in print</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Vivid RGB blue <code>#0000ff</code></td><td>Comes out noticeably purple</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bright green <code>#00ff00</code></td><td>Loses most of its intensity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Neon orange, hot pink</td><td>Flattens considerably</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anything fluorescent</td><td>Needs a spot ink. CMYK cannot print it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      No arithmetic can fix this. It is a physical limit of subtractive ink on reflective paper compared
      with additive light on a display.
    </p>

    <h2>Total ink coverage</h2>
    <p>
      The sum of the four percentages is the total area coverage. Most commercial presses cap it at
      240–320%, by paper stock. Above the cap, the sheet does not dry correctly and offsets onto the next
      one. If your converted values sum above 300%, ask the printer first. A rich black is usually
      about 60/40/40/100, which is 240%, and not 100/100/100/100.
    </p>

    <h2>What to send to a printer</h2>
    <p>
      Ask the printer first, because the answer varies. Most commercial printers prefer a PDF/X file with
      a declared output intent, and do the conversion with the correct profile. That gives a better
      result than a conversion with no profile. Use these numbers to set expectations, to match a brand
      colour approximately, and to fill in a form field that requires CMYK. For brand colours that must
      be exact across media, use a spot colour (Pantone).
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
